\ The targeted-inliner deliverable: the unmodified `30 fib .` builds under the
\ lock. `.` is a compound word (`[ inspect print-line ]`), so its call used to
\ flush the operand opaque and the inner `inspect` kept a cold interpreter
\ branch. The splicer inlines `.`'s body against the caller's typed stack, the
\ narrowed fixnum reaches `inspect`, and the site monomorphizes. The `dip`'d
\ `[ swap over + ]` likewise splices inline instead of compiling standalone
\ with opaque operands. A successful locked build is itself the proof.
\
\ fib's declared fixnum output is load-bearing twice over: it narrows the
\ entry's call-site result, and the freeze-time inference trusts it to prove
\ `.`'s parameter, which is what lets `.`'s standalone body monomorphize its
\ own `inspect` and pass the lock.

fib-step: ( a b n -- result ) [ dup 0 = [ drop drop ] [ 1 - [ swap over + ] dip fib-step ] if ] ;
fib: ( n -- result: fixnum ) [ dup 2 < [ ] [ 0 1 <rot- fib-step ] if ] ;

30 fib .
